"""Evidence-bounded continuous revision of the canonical manuscript."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,re,difflib
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
OUT=ROOT/'review-revision/html-review-implementation'
OLD=ROOT/'history/before-html-review-20261005'
log=[]
def replace(t,old,new,ids,file):
    assert t.count(old)==1,(ids,old[:90],t.count(old))
    log.append({'items':ids.split(','),'file':file,'before':old,'after':new})
    return t.replace(old,new)
def paragraph(t,start,new,ids,file):
    pos=t.index(start);end=t.find('\n\n',pos)
    if end<0:end=len(t)
    return replace(t,t[pos:end],new,ids,file)
def main():
    assert json.loads((OUT/'pre-edit-checks.json').read_text())['status']=='PASS_THREE_PRE_EDIT_CHECKS'
    m=(ROOT/'manuscript/main.tex').read_text();a=(ROOT/'manuscript/appendix.tex').read_text()
    assert m==(OLD/'manuscript/main.tex').read_text() and a==(OLD/'manuscript/appendix.tex').read_text()
    def M(old,new,ids):
        nonlocal m;m=replace(m,old,new,ids,'main.tex')
    def A(old,new,ids):
        nonlocal a;a=replace(a,old,new,ids,'appendix.tex')
    abstract=r'''Evaluator changes can alter which language outputs are selected. We study the score information needed to certify near-optimal retention under a budget on average membership changes. Score maps and the cutoff stay fixed across a finite family of uniformly weighted single-evaluator replacements. For mean and median pooling, partial scores yield a safe retained set and bound its count shortfall from the full-information optimum. For odd-sized median pools, we characterize minimum offline entry certificates: agreements establish feasibility, whereas oppositions exclude larger sets. Residual witnesses account for existing observations and mixed entry and whole-evaluator queries with fixed additive prices. Cached-score experiments on RewardBench~2 and five WMT panels separate required evidence from online discovery. At nominal 5\% selection and budget $.10$, exact median certification gives median reference-priority query-count ratios to offline minima of 1.968 on RewardBench and 1.662 on thirteen-stream WMT23, conditional on positive minima. Controlled panels isolate column sharing at fixed row costs. A paired control matches label-blind tie priorities across selection rules. These certificates establish stability and retained-count guarantees; output quality and execution savings require separate evidence.'''
    start=m.index(r'\begin{abstract}')+len(r'\begin{abstract}');end=m.index(r'\end{abstract}')
    M(m[start:end],'\n'+abstract+'\n\n','E001')
    intro=r'''Reward models and translation metrics select language outputs, but evaluator changes can alter the selected set \citep{malik2026rewardbench2,freitag2023wmt23,zheng2023judging}. Consider a batch selected by an evaluator pool and cutoff. A curator may retain some baseline selections and refer the remaining decisions for review. The trade-off is between retained count and sensitivity to evaluator replacement. Agreement alone does not establish semantic quality \citep{black2022selective,eisenstein2024herding}; review here interprets uncommitted decisions, without modeling human behavior or review cost.

We ask how much score evidence certifies this trade-off. Reference-fitted score maps and the cutoff remain fixed under single-evaluator changes from a declared finite family. One commitment mask is assessed across that family. The objective is to maximize returned baseline-selected count under a budget on average membership changes. This batch objective imposes neither a fixed-size quota nor one returned output per prompt, and gives no separate budget guarantee for each replacement.

The question has two connected parts: which observations suffice, and how many queries are needed to discover them? An offline certificate is chosen with knowledge of the true score matrix; an online policy sees only initially available information and earlier replies. For both mean and median pooling, partial scores determine safe and optimistic retained counts. Their gap certifies shortfall from the full-information optimum.

For odd-sized median pools, only selected boundary outputs can lose membership. Agreements certify feasibility; oppositions exclude larger sets. Their separation characterizes minimum entry certificates. Residual witnesses incorporate existing observations and shared columns under fixed additive prices. NLP replays compare discovery with the matching evidence minimum, while controlled panels vary column sharing at fixed row costs. Protocol controls change the selection task and are interpreted separately from same-task acquisition comparisons. Sections~\ref{sec:setting}--\ref{sec:median} develop the certificates; Sections~\ref{sec:acquisition}--\ref{sec:experiments} study their discovery.'''
    start=m.index(r'\section{Introduction}')+len(r'\section{Introduction}');end=m.index(r'\section{Related Work}')
    M(m[start:end],'\n'+intro+'\n\n','E002,I002')
    M(r'Conformal risk control (CRC) bounds expected monotone loss with parameter-dependent bounds \citep{angelopoulos2024crc,yeh2025conformal}.',r'Conformal risk control (CRC) bounds the expectation of a loss monotone in a selection parameter \citep{angelopoulos2024crc}; parameter-dependent bounds sharpen calibration \citep{yeh2025conformal}.','E003')
    M('SCoRE covers selected and marginal risk with zero-cost abstention','SCoRE controls selected and marginal risk for bounded losses with zero-cost abstention','E005')
    M('Covariance audits study shared quality errors','Covariance audits distinguish disagreement from shared quality errors','E006')
    M('Comparison-window certificates bound pipeline attribution error','Comparison-window certificates concern attribution error between evaluation pipelines','E007')
    M('Here unit-valued outputs have rescaled Boolean opposition costs; sorted prefixes determine optimal count. Entry or column observations certify this count or a specified gap.', 'In our median model, outputs have unit value and rescaled Boolean opposition costs. Sorted prefixes determine optimal retained count; entry or column observations certify that count or a specified safe--optimistic gap.','E008,E009,E010')
    M('NLP pruning, ensembles and ranking exits reduce scoring', 'Candidate-set pruning, adaptive ensembles and ranking exits reduce scoring in their respective settings','E136')
    M(r'Middleware top-$k$ assumes sorted and random access \citep{fagin2003optimal}.',r'Middleware top-$k$ uses sorted access and, in some variants, random access \citep{fagin2003optimal}; our entry oracle has no sorted-access interface.','E012')
    M('our queries reveal fixed scores.', 'our queries reveal fixed scores rather than samples of noisy rewards.','I001')
    M(r'Reference-fitted maps give normalized scores $s_e(i)\in[0,1]$ for evaluator $e$ and output $i$.', r'Reference-fitted maps give normalized scores $s_e(i)\in[0,1]$ for evaluator $e$ and output $i$. Here reference data fit the maps and cutoff; they are distinct from human reference translations.','E013')
    M(r'The true matrix, maps and cutoff $0<t_0\le1$ remain fixed.', r'Throughout certification, the unknown true matrix, fitted maps and cutoff $0<t_0\le1$ remain fixed.','E014')
    M('Let $f_{ir}$ count membership changes.', 'Let $f_{ir}$ count membership flips across those $M$ replacements.','E015')
    M('Baseline exclusions add no returned outputs. We commit them only when visible base scores rule out every membership flip under the declared replacements.', 'Baseline exclusions add no returned outputs, so we spend no queries certifying them. We commit an exclusion only when visible base scores rule out every membership flip under the declared replacements.','E017')
    M(r'Let $f_i(x)$ be the flip count when a candidate assigns score $x$ to output $i$, and let $p_i=\max_{x\in[0,1]}f_i(x)$.', r'Let $f_i(x)$ count flips across the $M$ replacements when a candidate assigns score $x$ to output $i$. Thus $f_{ir}=f_i(s_r(i))$ and $p_i=\max_{x\in[0,1]}f_i(x)$.','E020')
    M(r'The safe count $V^-=V(u)$ is feasible for every consistent completion; the optimistic count $V^+=V(\ell)$ is attainable in at least one.', r'Maximizing retention under $u$ yields a single mask of count $V^-=V(u)$ feasible for every consistent completion. The optimistic count $V^+=V(\ell)$ is the full-information optimum in at least one completion.','E021')
    M('The tight minimum-query formulas below use median Boolean costs; both pooling rules retain this count guarantee.', 'The minimum entry-certificate formulas below apply to median Boolean costs; retained-count bounds and stopping apply to both pooling rules.','E022')
    M(r'Choose integer $\varepsilon$ as the acceptable retained-count shortfall.', r'Choose a nonnegative integer $\varepsilon$ as the largest acceptable retained-count shortfall.','E024')
    M('A boundary row has exactly $h+1$ such scores.', 'A selected boundary row has exactly $h+1$ such scores.','E026')
    M('An observed agreement reduces the upper cost bound by one; an opposition increases the lower bound by one.', 'On a selected boundary row, an agreement (candidate score at or above the cutoff) reduces the upper opposition bound by one; a below-cutoff opposition increases the lower bound by one.','E027')
    M('An online policy must discover', 'An online policy must discover','E028') if False else None
    M(r'Given observations $\mathcal O$, the residual witness $Q_\varepsilon(\mathcal O)$ counts the minimum additional entries needed', r'Given observations $\mathcal O$, the residual witness $Q_\varepsilon(\mathcal O)$ counts the minimum additional entries needed for the same gap, accounting for existing agreements and oppositions','E033')
    M('The four strategies differ only in their next-query rule; interval updates, mask solver and stopping criterion are shared.', 'The certificate holds for any acquisition order. We compare four next-query rules with shared interval updates, mask solver and stopping criterion.','E035')
    M('Reference priority first selects the active row with the smallest estimated total flip cost, summing observed flips and predictions for unqueried entries.', 'Among active rows with at least one unqueried candidate score, reference priority selects the smallest estimated total flip cost: observed flips plus predictions for remaining entries.','E036')
    M('We compare initial retention, discovery and minimum evidence, then vary protocol and query unit.', 'We evaluate initial retention, online discovery relative to matching offline minima, and sensitivity to protocol and query unit. Quality is assessed separately.','E038')
    M('CRC and grouped SCoRE use calibration data.', 'CRC and grouped SCoRE use calibration data; the query certificate and reference-priority fit do not.','E041')
    M(r'Exact stopping uses zero gap. At $\alpha=.10$', r'Exact stopping uses zero gap. The guarantee is $V^*-K\le\varepsilon$, rather than 98\% coverage of $S_0$, since $V^*$ can be smaller than $|S_0|$. At $\alpha=.10$','E042')
    M('Overlapping splits supply descriptive panel summaries.', 'The overlapping splits give descriptive summaries, not independent replications for population inference.','E044')
    M('Counts start after panel construction, excluding full-panel WMT23 correlation reads.', 'Counts start after panel construction and exclude full-panel WMT23 preprocessing reads, including later test outputs. They do not measure acquisition savings from initial data collection.','E045')
    M('Coverage differences between baselines reflect different selection tasks.', 'Full-information controls vary the selection objective or guarantee; their coverage differences do not establish a better acquisition algorithm.','E046')
    M(r'Fixed-count retention changes the selection rule (Appendix~\ref{app:risk-interpretation}).', r'Fixed-count retention changes the selection rule; Appendix~\ref{app:baselines} additionally matches label-blind tie priorities in a paired full-information control.','I006')
    M(r'\paragraph{A calibrated same-loss comparison.}',r'\paragraph{Same loss with an expectation guarantee.}','E049')
    M('It uses fewer queries than each other strategy on 21/40 and 31/40 splits.', 'Reference priority uses strictly fewer queries than all three other strategies on 21/40 RewardBench and 31/40 thirteen-stream WMT23 splits.','E051')
    M('Depth changes the cutoff, selected rows and target; this is a descriptive comparison, not an isolated effect of prediction accuracy', 'Changing $q$ changes the cutoff, selected rows, nominal count $k$, flip allowance and stopping target; this does not isolate the effect of prediction accuracy','E053,E135')
    M(r'Official WMT23 MQM evaluates fixed masks post hoc (Appendix~\ref{app:quality}).', r'Official WMT23 MQM evaluates fixed masks post hoc. At median $q=.05$, only 28.3\% and 77.3\% of returned outputs have labels on thirteen and twelve streams; returned-minus-matched-label MQM differences are $-.0020$ and $-.0002$ (Appendix~\ref{app:quality}).','E062')
    # Acquisition granularity detail is already in Appendix K.2.
    # m=paragraph(m,'A column oracle reveals all $N$ scores',r'''A column query reveals all $N$ candidate scores, charging $N$ logical entries including invariant and nonselected outputs. The solver updates active selected rows. Fixed and random column orders use candidate index and a seeded permutation; reference order sums predicted upper-cost reductions with fixed-point ties (Appendix~\ref{app:mapping-column}). Its priority uses only reference and visible base information.''','E034,I004','main.tex')
    m=paragraph(m,'Required evidence and its discovery determine',r'''We studied the score evidence needed to certify near-optimal retention under a fixed replacement-average churn budget. Partial-score bounds give safe sets and retained-count guarantees for mean and median pooling. Median agreements and oppositions characterize minimum entry certificates; residual witnesses account for existing observations and fixed-price mixed queries. Online inspection must be interpreted against the matching minimum and initial retention. Sharing changes column requirements at fixed row costs, while protocol changes can alter the selection task itself. These results concern certified stability; quality and execution costs require separate evidence.''','E070,I004','main.tex')
    M('\\clearpage\n\\section*{Limitations}', r'\section*{Limitations}','F-EXTRA-01')
    M('The certificates cover uniformly weighted single-member replacements from the declared family.', 'The certificates cover uniformly weighted single-member replacements from the declared finite family, with fixed maps and cutoff. They bound replacement-average churn, not each replacement or evaluators outside the family.','E071')
    M('WMT24 results had not been inspected when fixing the risk objective and panel-inclusion criteria.', 'WMT24 results had not been inspected when fixing the risk objective and panel-inclusion criteria; query-priority numerics were repaired later after an exact-replay failure. This is a limited freeze, not a fully preregistered evaluation.','E071')
    # Appendix navigation links all extensions to the same certification question.
    A('\\raggedbottom\n',r'''\raggedbottom
\paragraph{Appendix guide.}
A proves the certificates; B--C specify replay and controls; D--J report retention, quality and risk comparisons; K--L examine protocol and loss definitions; M derives column, residual and mixed-query costs; N--P analyze tolerance, operational scope and prediction diagnostics.
''','I007')
    A('Sharpness is relative to this set.', 'Sharpness is relative to this set; it does not claim every endpoint assignment is realizable by a deployed evaluator.','E074')
    A('A constant worst-case ratio therefore requires additional assumptions.', 'Thus no deterministic exact strategy has a universal constant worst-case ratio to the offline minimum without additional assumptions.','E075')
    A(r'Safety requires at least $\max(0,LT-b)$ agreements by Theorem~\ref{thm:safe}.', r'Safety requires at least $\max(0,sT-b)$ agreements by Theorem~\ref{thm:safe}.','E076')
    A('For repeated result identifiers, the builder retains the last eligible occurrence.', r'''The pinned results revision is \texttt{b19c7033e964187d12e74a43a07f2d727a3d37e5}. Within each model JSON, the builder traverses the parallel \texttt{id}, \texttt{subset} and \texttt{scores} arrays in their native order and retains the last eligible occurrence of a repeated identifier. Common prompt IDs are then sorted numerically. The model/file mapping is \texttt{evidence/source-model-files.json}.''','E080')
    A('A results-dataset revision alone does not identify the corresponding model-weight checkpoints.', 'A results-dataset revision identifies the cached-score snapshot, not necessarily the model-weight checkpoints. Replaying scores requires the former; reproducing scorer execution additionally requires verified model revisions and interfaces.','E082')
    A('Reference ridge uses every reference item and its candidate flip count.', r'''For each candidate, ridge uses every reference output and its flip count for the current pool and cutoff. Features use ECDF-normalized base scores, with no additional feature standardization. The objective is the sum of squared residuals plus a squared $\ell_2$ penalty of coefficient 1, with an unpenalized intercept. The normal equations are solved with \texttt{numpy.linalg.solve}; the recorded replay uses NumPy 2.0.2. The original BLAS and platform versions are not established by that record.''','E083,I003')
    A('A separate full-matrix audit recomputes replies, rejects repeated queries and reconstructs the intervals and masks.', 'A separate audit uses the complete cached matrix to recompute replies, reject duplicate queries and reconstruct recorded intervals and masks.','E084')
    A('The diagnostic was specified after fixing the inspection protocol and is not used to tune acquisition or stopping.', 'This post hoc diagnostic leaves frozen fits, acquisition paths, stopping states and protocol unchanged. RewardBench and WMT23 informed development; this statement does not assert label-blind development history.','E089')
    A('The official metric scores agree numerically with all 173,784 metric-score entries of the frozen 24-metric, 557-segment, thirteen-stream panel.', r'''All 173,784 metric-score entries match the frozen 24-metric, 557-segment, thirteen-stream panel using strict equality of parsed Python float values, without a tolerance. This is numerical equality, not raw-file byte identity. The 44 official-file acquisition identities are in \texttt{review-revision/wmt23-official/source-manifest.json}.''','E090')
    A('The corresponding matched-label MQM differences are approximately $-.0020$ and $-.0002$.', 'The corresponding returned-minus-comparator mean MQM differences are approximately $-.0020$ and $-.0002$, respectively.','E092')
    A('The following trace illustrates how inspection order affects certification within one batch.', 'This single-batch trace illustrates an ordering difference in a configuration whose aggregate results favor largest width; it does not independently test why the difference occurs.','E093')
    A('Reference empirical maps depend on each evaluator and the reference output population, so their budget-independent values may be reused across budgets without using candidate-test scores.', 'For a fixed evaluator and reference-output population, ECDF maps can be reused across pools and budgets. Changing the base pool still requires refitting the consensus cutoff and ridge targets.','E096')
    A('The first third forms reference, the second forms a reserved calibration split not used by these WMT24 query experiments, and the remainder forms test.', r'''Documents are deduplicated in first-appearance order, shuffled with the split seed, and partitioned using $d_{\rm third}=\lfloor D/3\rfloor$. The first two partitions each contain $d_{\rm third}$ documents and the remainder forms test: 171 documents give 57/57/57 and 197 give 65/65/67. Documents remain intact; output counts depend on document lengths. The second partition is reserved for calibration and is not used by these WMT24 query experiments.''','E100')
    A('The RewardBench and WMT23 implementations are preserved.', 'RewardBench and WMT23 retain their original query-priority arithmetic; only WMT24 uses the fixed-point rule described here.','E101')
    A(r'For each returned mask we also compute the largest individual replacement churn and the fraction of replacements exceeding $\alpha$.', r'For each returned mask we report the largest replacement-specific churn and the fraction exceeding the numerical value $\alpha$. These are diagnostics, not certificate constraints.','E104')
    A('SCoRE accepts general bounded losses and provides both marginal and selected risk control', 'SCoRE provides marginal and selected risk control for general bounded losses','E105')
    A('These checks assess implementation consistency; they neither replace the published theorem nor establish group exchangeability.', 'These finite checks test implementation consistency; they neither prove the published risk theorem nor establish exchangeability of the benchmark groups.','E106')
    A('These exceedances do not by themselves contradict the expectation guarantee.', 'An expectation guarantee does not require every batch to meet its budget. These exceedances therefore do not contradict the marginal expectation guarantee under the stated exchangeability assumptions.','E107')
    A('The 40 separately refitted, overlapping splits do not form such a deployment sequence.', 'The 40 overlapping, separately refitted splits do not evaluate batches sharing one frozen setup and therefore do not measure deployment amortization.','E108')
    A('The nominal $2k$ scale equals the largest symmetric difference between two lists of size $k$.', r'For two $k$-element subsets of an $N$-output batch, the largest symmetric difference is $2\min(k,N-k)$. The nominal $2k$ scale is a general upper bound, attained when $2k\le N$.','E112')
    A(r'Extra observations cannot enlarge the capacity gap, and at most $Q_{\rm exact}$ columns are touched.', r'Extra observations cannot decrease safe capacity or increase optimistic capacity, so they cannot enlarge the gap; at most $Q_{\rm exact}$ columns are touched.','E118')
    A('A cardinality/true-cost dynamic program computes the minimum.',r'''A cardinality/true-cost dynamic program computes the minimum. Let $D_j(s,c)$ be the minimum upper-cost sum for $s$ rows among the first $j$ with true cost exactly $c$. Initialize $D_0(0,0)=0$ and all other states to $+\infty$. Then
\[
 D_j(s,c)=\min\{D_{j-1}(s,c),
 D_{j-1}(s-1,c-z_j)+u_j\},
\]
omitting infeasible indices, and
\[
 G_s(\mathcal O)=\max\{0,\min_{0\le c\le b}D_m(s,c)-b\}.
\]
The cost dimension can be capped at $\min(b,mT)$. A dense implementation takes $O(m(L^*+1)(\min(b,mT)+1))$ time; the supplied implementation stores only reachable states. This is an offline computation using true costs $z_i$, not an acquisition policy.''','E119')
    A(r'Any successful gap certificate has a safe count $s\in[\max(0,L^*-\varepsilon),L^*]$ and excludes $s+\varepsilon+1$ rows.', r'Any successful gap certificate has safe count $s\in[\max(0,L^*-\varepsilon),L^*]$. If $p=s+\varepsilon+1\le m$, it must prove no $p$-row set feasible under any consistent completion. If $p>m$, no exclusion evidence is needed.','E120')
    A('even the fewest-column minimum-cost witness uses more columns than the minimum feasible count.', 'every minimum-cost witness uses more columns than the minimum feasible column count, including after breaking cost ties in favor of fewer columns.','E123')
    A(r'The nominal count is $k=m$ and rational $\alpha=2b/(7mT)$ gives flip budget exactly $4b$.', r'The nominal count is $k=m$, consistent with $q=m/(m+48)$ in the general definition, and rational $\alpha=2b/(7mT)$ gives flip budget exactly $4b$.','E125')
    A('Scores include both cutoff ties and strict inequalities.', r'''Base low and high values are drawn from $U(.1,.3)$ and $U(.65,.95)$. Selected boundary, selected interior, invariant unselected and unselected boundary rows have respectively four, five, two and three high votes. Selected boundary rows include designated first-member ties at $.5$. Their candidate opposition and agreement scores are $.25$ and $.75$, with designated agreements tied at $.5$; other rows have candidate scores from $U(.1,.95)$. The complete tie indices, random-number order and separate reference seeds are specified by \texttt{make\_panel} in \texttt{scripts/run\_synthetic\_certificates.py}.''','E124')
    A(r'Saved records retain the field name \texttt{tau} for this fraction;', r'Saved records retain the legacy field \texttt{tau} for $\eta$, as mapped in \texttt{RESULT\_DATA\_DICTIONARY.md};','E126')
    A('It is not an unbiased initial error or a causal explanation of query cost.', 'Because the rows and prefix depend on acquisition and stopping, this diagnostic is not an independent measure of initial prediction accuracy or a causal explanation of query cost.','E133')
    A('reconstruct every saved stopping count bounds.', 'reconstruct the retained-count bounds at every saved stopping state.','E134')
    # Move arithmetic after the split/map specification, rather than duplicating it.
    start=a.index(r'\paragraph{Numerical consistency.}');end=a.index(r'\paragraph{Panel construction.}')
    block=a[start:end];a=a[:start]+a[end:]
    pos=a.index('Safe retention and query priorities use');a=a[:pos]+block+a[pos:]
    log.append({'items':['E079'],'file':'appendix.tex','operation':'Move unchanged arithmetic paragraph after panel and split/map specification'})
    for name,t in [('main.tex',m),('appendix.tex',a)]:
        (ROOT/'manuscript'/name).write_text(t)
        old=(OLD/'manuscript'/name).read_text()
        (OUT/(name+'.diff')).write_text(''.join(difflib.unified_diff(old.splitlines(True),t.splitlines(True),fromfile='before/'+name,tofile='after/'+name)))
    (OUT/'edit-log.json').write_text(json.dumps(log,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
    print(json.dumps({'operations':len(log),'items_with_direct_edits':len(set(x for r in log for x in r['items']))}))
